AUDIO: 'What\'s going on everybody? Lord Tech here with another video for you guys. And today we are going to be testing the new One Animate 2 model that has released within the past few days. But we\'re not only going to be testing this model and seeing how good it is as an upgrade over the original One Animate'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that came out almost a year ago now. We\'re also going to be comparing it to what I consider the current king of motion control within inside ComfyUI. So One Animate 2 came out on August 7th. The default template is already sitting'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'natively in ComfyUI. You can just head over to the workflow template section and get it installed. But I do want to warn you guys, it is very bare bones and there is a lot of manual node processing that you have to do in order to get the workflow to work properly. I\'m sure node'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'support will come out soon for it, but as of right now there is quite a bit that you have to do to actually get the output that most of you guys are going to be looking for. Real quick guys, before we get into the video, I just want to take a minute to shout out my'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Discord and Patreon. If you want direct guidance or a custom workflow or a custom node built for you, I\'ve got a brand new system for that now. That\'s on top of the free workflows already sitting in the server, no strings attached, ready for you to download. I also run giveaways in there regularly.'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Plus there\'s a ton of other channels like a support channel where a 2000 plus person community of members are helping each other troubleshoot in real time. And if you guys want to support me even further, my Patreon\'s got exclusive workflows and node packs you won\'t find'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anywhere else. Like this insanely customizable prompt builder node. It\'s honestly one of the most useful things I\'ve ever made. And there\'s also the metadata forge which strips and swaps your exit data so every image looks camera authentic for your social media'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'posts. I really appreciate it guys. Thank you for taking the time to support me and let\'s get into the video. Today I\'m going to be testing Animate 2 against Scale 2 which has quietly taken the spot as king for motion control in ComfyUI over the past 2 months. So we'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are going to see if Animate 2 is actually a threat or just another model drop. So here\'s what\'s actually different about this one. One Animate 1 ran your video through a pose extractor first. So it would use Bit Pose to get a skeleton key point and then map that'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'across all the frames of the generation, and that skeleton obviously drove the movement of the character. But, that exact extraction is where you lost a lot of quality, such as finger position, subtle expression, anything the skeleton model couldn\'t represent was just kind'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of gone before the generation even started. And Animate 2 skips that entirely. The driving video frames go straight into the transformer, so there\'s no skeleton in between, and that\'s the whole pitch about Animate 2, and it\'s why I wanted to test it against hands and face specifically. So, right'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out of the box, there\'s two new nodes shipping with Animate 2. There is the 1 Animate 2 2 Video, which takes your reference image and your driving video and conditions on both at once. And I\'m pretty sure the other node that shipped'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with Animate 2 is the 1 Animate 2 Cache, which is optional. It caches the pose branch, so you\'re not recomputing it every step. I I think it cuts your generation down by some time, but I\'m not sure how effective it actually is. So, one important thing worth noting'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before you touch this workflow is that the output background isn\'t your reference photo, and it isn\'t your driving video. It\'s whatever you write in the prompt. Now, if you guys have used 1 Animate version 1 in the past, you know that it takes the background'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from the reference video usually, but 1 Animate 2 does not do this. It\'s fully based on the conditioning of the prompt and completely decoupled from both the reference image and the reference video. So, if you skip the background line in your prompt, you\'re just getting'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'whatever the model decides to generate, which may look like the background of the reference photo or video, but it can definitely cause some issues. And that\'s actually the reason that I built the front half of this workflow up a little bit. So, instead of typing the prompt by hand every time, I\'ve got Gemini 4'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reading the reference image and writing it for me. So, Gemini\'s outputting two lines every time. One\'s going to be the character description, and then one\'s going to be the background description straight off of the photo. And it reads both of those from the reference photo. And you\'ll notice these gigantic nodes'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'over here, which are basically just subgraphs that have a bunch of the actual video processing nodes inside of them. But, what I actually did is I wired up two LoRA loader stacks inside of these subgraphs. So, if you take a'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look inside of the actual subgraph and you\'re using the workflow that I\'ve modified, which you can find on my Discord for free, by the way. It\'s going to be in the free workflow section in the Discord, and the link to that is in the description. By default, this workflow only had a single Laura input,'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which is obviously going to be taken up by the light X2V turbo Laura, which makes the animate model run a lot faster. But, with these two Laura stacks loaded in our model loader pipeline, you can take a look here and see that you'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have the ability to add a ton of Lauras. And I\'m not going to go through every single one of them, but if you\'re interested, I have created a markdown note on the workflow where you can go and get these models. The links to all of them are right there. I definitely'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recommend getting some of these Lauras. A lot of them help with quality. Some of them help with other things, which I\'ll let you read on screen. And none of that ships in the stock template, so I figured I would help you guys out by adding a little bit of quality of life'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before all of the programmers who make extensive node packs get on to it. And we\'re going to get right into some more generations, but I just want to point out that this workflow is kind of annoying to use. And that is because each one of these giant green subgraphs'

[04:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on screen right now only generates 81 frames, and that\'s only going to be about two and a half seconds if you\'re using a reference video that is running 30 frames per second. So, if your driving video is longer than two and a half seconds, what you have to do is'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'enable another subgraph. And let\'s say your [snorts] video is longer than five seconds. Well, you need to enable another subgraph. And let\'s say your video is longer than seven and a half seconds. Well, you\'re going to have to copy and paste and rewire another subgraph onto this workflow. Or you can'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just accept the fact that your video generations will not be longer than seven and a half seconds even if the reference video is longer. And we can see here that the default template actually ships with this math equation'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that tells you how many subgraphs you\'re going to need based on the length of the video that you\'re using in the frames. You can see here, native loop support public repository is still under review, so you have to manually copy and paste the motion transfer subgraph in order to'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make a longer video. The number here indicates how many subgraphs you need to match the pose reference video length. So, if I just run up to this node real quick, we can see in order for this workflow to generate the entirety of the'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'reference video that I currently have put into the workflow, I\'m going to need at least 2.7 subgraphs. Now, we can see here that I currently have three, and they\'re all enabled. But, let\'s say you only have a 5-second video. What you can do is just bypass the last subgraph and'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take the third image output out of the batch images node. But, like I said, it\'s quite tedious, and it\'s definitely not beginner-friendly as of now. There definitely will be nodes for going on in the future, and a lot of the node packs'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that have been known to release one video-related nodes will definitely be updated soon in the future. But, for now, this is what we need to do. All right, so let\'s actually run it. Let\'s use the same reference image, the same'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'driving clip, and test both pipe blind scale two and animate two. We\'re going to be generating the same seed and the same resolution, and so on. And, I\'ll compare the generation times as well, so we can see which model\'s kind of faster and which model we\'re getting better'

[06:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
